{"type":"text","timestamp":1794307141665,"sessionID":"ses_e3afb8e6dffemZh34YunfKALwC","part":{"id":"prt_1c50a2fff001t2JsLJsDA3xFlk","messageID":"msg_1c50a1e2c001fx1hykqUtmjhav","sessionID":"ses_e3afb8e6dffemZh34YunfKALwC","type":"text","text":"<think>No browser available. Installing Playwright browsers requires downloading Chromium (~150MB) from the network — the sandbox has restricted network access (only package registries). `npm i playwright` would install the package but the browser download comes from a CDN, likely blocked. I could try `npx playwright install chromium` and see. It might work via the proxy (HTTPS_PROXY is set to llm-gateway). Let me try quickly — a browser smoke test would be valuable to verify the UI actually works.\n\nLet me try installing playwright in a temp location inside the workspace (not committed). Actually, node_modules in /workspace/frontend is gitignored? There's no .gitignore yet — I'll create one. Let me try installing playwright as a dev dependency in a separate directory to avoid polluting the frontend package.json/lock.\n\nI'll create /workspace/.e2e/ with its own package.json, install playwright there, and try to download chromium. If the download fails, I'll fall back to reasoning + a jsdom-based check.\n\nActually, jsdom-based testing of a React app with fetch mocking is complex. Let me first try playwright — worth ~1 minute.\n\nLet me check network access to the playwright CDN via the proxy.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794307141631,"end":1794307141661}}}
